WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:08.940
Aerial archaeologists take to the skies over
remote wooded hills in central Norway.

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:16.900
An on board camera fires laser pulses at the
ground to produce a LiDAR image of what lies

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:19.270
hidden beneath the icy landscape.

00:00:22.330 --> 00:00:27.580
This LIDAR image really reveals some
incredible structures on the ground here.

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:35.100
We're seeing all these strange formations,
circles and what look like three pointed

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:37.950
stars. The whole thing looks so weird.

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:40.740
It looks like something out of a cell
biology textbook.

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:49.340
The aerial image has uncovered dozens of
structures lost to history.

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:52.260
They're clearly man made.

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:57.960
And so that makes me wonder, how old are
these things and who made them and why?

00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:05.380
Archaeologist David Hill is heading to the
site to investigate.

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:12.550
I'm very excited about the structures that
you can see on the LiDAR image.

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:15.190
Some of them I really can't explain.

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:16.900
I haven't seen them before.

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:23.170
Hill thinks some of the structures in the
image are longhouses.

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:27.770
Timber buildings built over 1000 years ago.

00:01:29.630 --> 00:01:35.180
It suggests the mystery shapes are legacies
of the Norse warriors who rose from these

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:37.730
lands at the end of the eighth century ad.

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:43.050
I suspect they might be Vikings.

00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:48.670
The thing that I'm really unsure about,
though, is the star shaped formation.

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:50.770
I honestly don't know what they are.

00:01:58.310 --> 00:01:59.840
All right. We seem to be getting up to the
site.

00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:13.870
According to the LiDAR data, deep beneath
this undulating land lies a vast array of

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:15.610
circles and stars.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:21.600
Absolutely fantastic.

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:29.920
Many, many sites they've been altered, been
plowed over, but not here.

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:31.350
What we're seeing is intact.

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:38.790
Several of the circular structures are poking
through the earth.

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:41.160
More evidence.

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:44.460
Hill believes that this is a Viking site.

00:02:46.030 --> 00:02:47.800
It seems to be quite a well defined.

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:53.190
Ditch. I think this is a burial mound.

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:57.180
Ditches were very important.

00:02:57.210 --> 00:03:00.470
They delineate the sacred area within the
grave mound.

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:02.580
It's the realm of the dead outside.

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:03.900
It's the realm of the living.

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:05.910
So I'm now stepping into the realm of the
dead.

00:03:09.460 --> 00:03:13.090
What we're seeing is a burial site from the
Viking period.

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:22.400
The image suggests this realm of the dead
contains the remains of over 100 Vikings.

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:28.990
You can see. One, two, three, four, five,
six.

00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:31.810
I can't even count them.

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:35.210
This site is incredible.

00:03:37.970 --> 00:03:43.370
Fiercely pagan, the Norsemen would often
cremate their dead in the belief that the

00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:45.620
smoke would carry them to the afterlife.

00:03:46.970 --> 00:03:51.290
For others, burial mounds are seen as
gateways to another world.

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:57.660
One inhabited by those who had died before
them and the gods they worshipped yet also

00:03:57.660 --> 00:03:58.380
feared.

00:04:02.010 --> 00:04:04.860
What these mounds represent is the cosmology
of the Vikings.

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:12.120
This is where the living would commune with
the ancestors and the gods.

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:17.390
Wow. Look at that.

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:18.950
Up there on the ridge.

00:04:21.080 --> 00:04:25.640
Two carved slabs of granite crowned the
largest of the burial mounds.

00:04:28.270 --> 00:04:30.750
This is an.

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:37.310
Enormous. Stone of this size would have been
certainly for somebody very important.

00:04:40.050 --> 00:04:44.760
The Norsemen would often inscribe gravestones
like this with runes.

00:04:45.490 --> 00:04:50.650
Mystical symbols which they believed were
originally discovered by the great God, Odin.

00:04:52.630 --> 00:04:57.280
They could be used as a means of
communication between this world and the next

00:04:57.280 --> 00:04:58.930
or as spells.

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:04.590
The stone was built to convene with the gods.

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:08.610
The spirit. Well, it was raised as a gift to
them.

00:05:11.650 --> 00:05:16.750
According to Norse beliefs, half the warriors
killed in battle go to Valhalla.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:23.640
The rest head to Folkvangr ruled over by the
goddess of sex and war.

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:33.500
Freya. And recent research suggests the
Vikings desire to appease their gods as they

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:38.150
prepare for these journeys has its origins
in a real historical event.

00:05:39.140 --> 00:05:46.400
We've got a lot of tantalizing clues that at
the end of 535 ad, there was a massive famine

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:48.740
and millions of people died all across
Europe.

00:05:51.260 --> 00:05:57.620
Geological records reveal that in that year,
dust from a massive volcanic eruption plunges

00:05:57.620 --> 00:05:59.810
Europe into months of darkness.

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:07.070
Causing crops to fail and killing a third of
the entire population of Scandinavia.

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:12.180
All those natural phenomena that we today
understand by science.

00:06:14.020 --> 00:06:17.290
Back in the Viking age, they would
understand by relating the gods to them.

00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:27.420
As stories of this catastrophe are passed
down over generations, it inspires the tales

00:06:27.420 --> 00:06:32.580
of destruction and vengeful gods that
dominate Viking myths and sagas.

00:06:34.820 --> 00:06:38.630
In mythology, this is described as Ragnarok
or the Apocalypse.

00:06:38.630 --> 00:06:43.880
And when that happens, things go dark and it
takes several hundred years for the

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:45.860
population to build back up.

00:06:48.230 --> 00:06:53.750
What puzzles Hill is how the giant star
shapes in the image are connected to the

00:06:53.750 --> 00:06:56.180
Vikings beliefs in their gods and the
afterlife.

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:03.810
This is without doubt the strangest thing
I've seen in archaeology.

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:08.880
I'm walking down one of the arms of the star
shaped structure.

00:07:09.270 --> 00:07:13.320
Remarkable. Absolutely remarkable.

00:07:16.650 --> 00:07:20.940
Hill knows that three was a magical number
for the Vikings.

00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:27.390
The arms have to mean something.

00:07:28.110 --> 00:07:31.830
Odin, the God who created the first humans,
was one of three brothers.

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:36.630
And before the Viking apocalypse.

00:07:36.630 --> 00:07:39.360
Ragnarok. There were three winters without
summers.

00:07:41.050 --> 00:07:42.820
What's more, the God.

00:07:42.820 --> 00:07:44.710
Loki has three children.

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:47.280
Were three weapons.

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:53.520
And Fenrir, The monstrous Wolf of the
apocalypse, is bound by three chains.

00:07:57.060 --> 00:08:02.550
This is clearly a link between the world in
which we live and the realm of the gods.

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:07.730
What went through the the minds of the
people who built this.

